\title{Overwintering Survival of Three Cover Crops\ under Reduced Tillage}
\author{L.~Petrova and M.~Okafor}
\date{March 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We report first-year overwintering survival of three cover crop
species --- winter rye, hairy vetch, and crimson clover --- under
reduced tillage on a silty clay loam in the lower Fraser Valley.
Survival was scored at green-up on forty-eight plots across two
sowing dates. Rye survived best, clover worst, and the late sowing
depressed survival of the two legumes by roughly ten percentage
points.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Cover crops are increasingly promoted as a tool for retaining
soil nitrogen and protecting structure over the wet winter months,
yet their value depends on surviving until spring.
In maritime climates the limiting factors are waterlogging and
freeze--thaw heaving rather than deep cold alone.
This note summarises the first year of a small on-farm trial
comparing three widely used species under reduced tillage.

\section{Trial Design}
Forty-eight plots of 3~m by 10~m were laid out in a randomised
complete block design with four blocks.
Each block contained the three species crossed with two sowing
dates (early and late September) and two replicate plots per
combination.
Tillage was reduced to a single shallow pass before sowing;
no fertiliser was applied.
Survival was scored at green-up in late February as the
percentage of emerged plants relative to the autumn stand count.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Species & Early sown survival (\%) & Late sown survival (\%) \\
\midrule
Winter rye      & 97 & 94 \\
Hairy vetch     & 88 & 79 \\
Crimson clover  & 76 & 66 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Overwintering survival by species and sowing date}
\label{tab:survival}
\end{table}

\section{Preliminary Results}
Table~\ref{tab:survival} shows that rye was nearly insensitive
to sowing date, whereas both legumes lost about ten percentage
points of survival with the late sowing.
Plot-level survival $S$ was computed as
\begin{equation}
S = 100 \times \frac{N_{\mathrm{spring}}}{N_{\mathrm{autumn}}}
\end{equation}
where $N$ denotes the stand count in each season.

\section{Next Steps}
The trial continues for two further winters.
Spring biomass and nitrogen uptake will be reported once the
laboratory analyses return, and a second site on a lighter soil
is planned for next season.

\end{document}
